      "writtenOn": "2023-01-24T00:53:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If the requested duty is clamped to higher or lower value than the host expected, do we need to inform the DPTF daemon? Clamping up is fine but clamping down can cause a problem?\n\nI couldn\u0027t find the definition of \u0027duty cycle\u0027 in Intel\u0027s DPTF docs. Does it mean the PWM duty or the ratio between min_fan_speed and max_fan_speed?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 190,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 195,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "0b7946592bff1d8308f4060316641f707bb1c727",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "68169d36_b8b93adf",
        "filename": "common/fan.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 195,
      "author": {
        "id": 1365384
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-01-24T15:44:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is no need to inform dptf on if the requested duty is higher or lower. Just that , we have to take care that , they are  set within the proper limit, in case if it is improper value.\nClamping up is fine.\nBut clamping down will result in stalling the fan(which is how this bug appeared for brya).\nThe duty cycle here corresponds to PWM duty. For instance , in brya, this is set in NPCX\u0027s DCR register (pwm_set_raw_duty() )",
      "parentUuid": "70052ae6_5b67635a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 190,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 195,
        "endChar": 0
      },
